---
/**
 * The real app, photographed.
 *
 * Everything else on this page that looks like macOS is drawn in HTML —
 * `Hero.astro`'s menu bar and its two Activity Monitor panels, the `make audit`
 * transcript in `Rules.astro`, the fan-in diagram in `Problem.astro` — and each
 * of those is drawn on purpose: it shows something a capture cannot photograph
 * at all (a menu-bar popover is a high-layer panel, not an ordinary window), or
 * two states at once, or it has to reflow on a phone in a way an image cannot.
 * This section is the other half of that arrangement: the window as it actually
 * renders, so a reader can check the drawings against it.
 *
 * The images are NOT hand-placed. `make screenshots` from the repository root
 * builds Bastion, launches it in demo mode onto each screen, gates the result
 * against committed goldens, and writes the files into src/assets/shots — which
 * that command CLEARS on every run. Do not park anything else in that
 * directory, and do not edit these files: the next capture overwrites them. To
 * change what a shot shows, change the fixture in
 * apps/apple/Bastion/DemoSeed.swift.
 *
 * Imported through astro:assets rather than referenced out of public/, for two
 * reasons: these are 2360px PNGs that would otherwise ship unoptimised, and a
 * missing capture then fails `astro build` outright instead of building clean
 * and 404ing in production.
 */
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import chatShot from "../assets/shots/chat.png";
import clientShot from "../assets/shots/client.png";
import licenceShot from "../assets/shots/licence.png";
import logShot from "../assets/shots/log.png";
import runningShot from "../assets/shots/running.png";
import serverShot from "../assets/shots/server.png";
import SectionHeader from "./SectionHeader.astro";

/**
 * The captions are deliberately NOT the ones in
 * apps/apple/Screenshots/screenshots.config.json. That file captions the framed
 * store-shaped plates, which are read alone and out of context and therefore
 * have to say what the app *is*; here the section header has already done that,
 * so these say what each particular pane answers. The two rot separately and
 * should.
 *
 * `id` is the capture's name; the anchor it becomes is prefixed `shot-`. The
 * bare `#servers`, `#status`, `#rules` and `#how` are taken by sections the nav
 * links to, and an unprefixed id here would be a second element answering to a
 * name something already navigates to.
 */
const shots = [
  {
    image: runningShot,
    id: "running",
    title: "One process per profile",
    body: "Four supervised children for five profiles — staging/shopify is still a credential short, so it has never started. Each row carries the clients attached to it, by the identity its token was issued to and by whatever it calls itself, because the interesting case is when those two disagree.",
    alt: "Bastion's Running pane: prod/shopify up two hours with two clients attached, acme/keycloak up 47 minutes, home/unifi-network stopped, and lab/unifi-network with a writes badge and restarted twice.",
  },
  {
    image: serverShot,
    id: "server",
    title: "Two profiles, one installed server",
    body: "A profile is a named credential set, so the same server can serve two accounts without either knowing about the other. prod is running; staging says exactly which variable it is waiting for. The environment table underneath is the server's own, read from the manifest rather than typed here.",
    alt: "Bastion's Shopify pane: the installed package at 1.4.2, a running prod profile and a staging profile reading “cannot start — missing SHOPIFY_CLIENT_SECRET”, above the server's environment variables with required and secret badges.",
  },
  {
    image: logShot,
    id: "log",
    title: "The log, with the arguments",
    body: "Not just which tool — what it was called with, and what came back for a profile that opts in. Credentials are redacted before the row is written rather than filtered out of it afterwards, which is why one entry here reads «redacted» where a client secret would have been. None of it is written to disk.",
    alt: "Bastion's Log pane: timestamped calls across prod/shopify, acme/keycloak and home/unifi-network, each with its JSON arguments and result beneath it, and one row whose client_secret argument reads «redacted».",
  },
  {
    image: clientShot,
    id: "client",
    title: "The file it is about to write",
    body: "One entry per profile, each carrying a bearer token issued to that client and never a credential — that split is what makes writing into somebody else's config defensible at all. The servers you configured by hand are listed underneath, untouched, with the note that nothing they do reaches the log.",
    alt: "Bastion's Claude Code pane: the path to .claude.json, five entries pointing at 127.0.0.1:8720 with configured and not-written badges, and a card listing two hand-configured servers the file already held.",
  },
  {
    image: chatShot,
    id: "chat",
    title: "Try a tool before wiring it up",
    body: "One profile's tools loaded into the on-device model, with the arguments it invents and the results it gets shown inline. The header counts what is loaded against the context it costs, because a server with forty tools does not fit and the pane would rather say so than quietly drop some.",
    alt: "Bastion's Chat pane: two questions answered against prod/shopify, each showing the tool called, its arguments and its JSON result, with six of fourteen tools loaded at 213 of 1800 tokens.",
  },
  {
    image: licenceShot,
    id: "licence",
    title: "A trial that is the whole app",
    body: "Thirty minutes, every server relaying and every write gate obeying its own switch. It answers whether this works on your Mac against your servers, which is a different question from whether it was worth the money — and that one is what the refund is for.",
    alt: "Bastion's Licence settings: a trial with 28 minutes left, the two sentences explaining what runs during it and what stops after, and an empty licence key field.",
  },
] as const;

/**
 * Every plate is rendered at the SAME pixel scale, which is not what `w-full`
 * on its own gives you.
 *
 * Five of these are the main window at 2360px; `licence` is the Settings
 * window, which is genuinely a smaller window and comes out of the capture at
 * 1520. Stretched to a shared column width it rendered about half again as
 * large as the others — same traffic lights, same 13pt type, visibly bigger —
 * and a set of screenshots at two scales reads as two applications.
 *
 * So the column is 1180 CSS px for the widest capture and each other plate
 * takes its share of that, centred. `sizes` has to follow, or the browser picks
 * a candidate for a width the image is never rendered at.
 */
const COLUMN = 1180;
const widest = Math.max(...shots.map((shot) => shot.image.width));

const plate = (image: (typeof shots)[number]["image"]) => {
  const share = image.width / widest;
  const css = Math.round(COLUMN * share);
  return {
    style: `max-width:${css}px`,
    widths: [Math.round(css * 0.65), css, css * 2],
    sizes: `(min-width: 1240px) ${css}px, ${Math.round(share * 100)}vw`,
  };
};
---

<section id="screens" class="scroll-mt-20 border-t border-rule py-22">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-start gap-16">
      <SectionHeader eyebrow="The app" heading="A window, not a background process.">
        <p class="m-0 text-[16px] leading-[1.6] text-fg/58 text-pretty">
          Holding the servers in one signed process rather than spawning them inside whichever
          editor asked first is what makes any of this visible: there is somewhere to look, and it
          says what happened. These are captures of that window, taken from a real build by
          <code class="font-mono text-[14px] text-fg/75">make screenshots</code> and checked against
          committed goldens, so they cannot quietly fall behind the app.
        </p>
      </SectionHeader>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16 flex flex-col gap-14">
      {
        shots.map(({ image, id, title, body, alt }) => (
          <figure id={`shot-${id}`} class="reveal m-0 scroll-mt-24">
            {/* Widths are the rendered CSS widths, not the source's: the plate
                is drawn at `css` and the largest candidate is its 2x. avif then
                webp, with the PNG as the fallback the <picture> keeps. */}
            <Picture
              src={image}
              alt={alt}
              widths={plate(image).widths}
              sizes={plate(image).sizes}
              formats={["avif", "webp"]}
              fallbackFormat="png"
              style={plate(image).style}
              class="mx-auto block h-auto w-full rounded-[14px] border border-rule-2 shadow-[0_24px_60px_-34px_rgb(0_0_0/90%)]"
            />
            <figcaption class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap items-start gap-x-16 gap-y-2">
              <h3 class="m-0 flex-1 basis-[456px] font-display text-[20px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-fg">
                {title}
              </h3>
              <p class="m-0 flex-1 basis-[456px] text-[14.5px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
                {body}
              </p>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
